      "loreText": "A voice memo recovered from Vikram's iCloud backup. Timestamped 11:42 PM, two nights before his death. He is alone, the audio is close-mic'd, occasional sounds of a pen tapping against a desk. The transcript reads: 'Okay. Okay. The Thakur thing keeps coming back. I can't make the episode work without it but I can't prove it. Bisht's land — that's clean on paper, all the registry stuff routes through shell companies but the timeline lines up too well, it's not a coincidence. And the rifle. The rifle is the rifle, I'm sure of it, I just can't say so on camera without — [pause] — Anya knows. Anya knows something. She won't sit down with me. Three times now. She's not avoiding me, she's avoiding the question. Hm. [pause] Rhea is going to be a problem when this drops. She'll see the numbers in the edit suite before anyone else does and she'll know I know. Channel sale, she thinks I haven't noticed, I've noticed. Whatever. [pause] Naina — I owe Naina a call. I owe her the call. She did the thing and I haven't even — god. [pause] Kabir, god, Kabir. The thesis thing is a sideshow but if I name him it'll come up and his career is — I haven't decided. I haven't decided. [pause] Devraj. Three pages. Three pages on a death investigation. He didn't even — [audio cuts off]' The recording ends abruptly. Battery log shows the phone hit 1 percent at that timestamp. The memo is filed in his Voice Memos app under no specific name — it appears next to dozens of other late-night thinking-aloud recordings he had made over the previous six months. This is just how he worked.",
