      "description": "CBI scene photographs of a corkboard covering one wall of Vikram's study. Three names on the spine. One name in the corner. Red string between some. Question marks over others.",
      "loreText": "Four overlapping crime-scene photographs of the cottage's back wall. The board is the standard set-up of a working investigative journalist — pinned printouts, marker scribbles, Instagram screenshots dry-mounted with masking tape, lengths of red cotton thread connecting pins. Three names are written in block capitals across the top of the main panel: BISHT. DEVRAJ. ANYA. Under each, one or two pieces of pinned material and a phrase in Vikram's handwriting. Under BISHT: a long-lens photograph of an antique rifle mounted on a wall and the words 'TROPHY?? — see thakur'; pinned next to it, a newspaper cutting headlined 'THAKUR FAMILY MURDERED IN COTTAGE ROBBERY — Doon Echo, Dec 2011'. Under DEVRAJ: a photocopy of the three-page police report with one paragraph circled and 'why so thin' in the margin; a second pinned cutting on Devraj's promotion to inspector in 2014. Red string runs from BISHT to the Doon Echo cutting and from DEVRAJ to the same cutting — but no string runs between BISHT and DEVRAJ themselves. Vikram had not yet found the 8 PM call. Under ANYA: a single Instagram still of the Grey Lady and a fat question mark in marker; no string at all. The marginalia under her name reads only 'won't sit'. To the right of the main panel, separated by a thin line of black tape, is a smaller corner of the board labelled in pencil 'LOOSE THREADS'. Inside this corner: KABIR's name on a sticky note, next to a screenshot of an academic-misconduct policy and the words 'thesis — decide'. There is no string from the corner to anything else. The wall is a working document, not an answer key. The Thakur spine is three names solid; Kabir is a maybe; the connection between the two men who actually killed Vikram is the one line Vikram never drew.",
